# Why Use TypeScript Instead of JavaScript?

TypeScript is a statically typed superset of JavaScript developed by Microsoft that compiles down to plain JavaScript.

---

## 1. Core Advantages

1. **Catch Bugs at Compile Time:** Detect null pointers, typos in property names, and incorrect argument signatures before code ever ships to production.
2. **Self-Documenting Code & Contracts:** Types act as living, compiler-verified documentation for teams and APIs.
3. **Refactoring Confidence:** Safely rename fields, modify function signatures, and refactor architecture across hundreds of files with immediate compiler feedback.
4. **Superior IDE Autocompletion (IntelliSense):** Rich code navigation, parameter hints, and instantaneous jump-to-definition.

```typescript
interface User {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  isActive: boolean;
}

function sendWelcomeEmail(user: User) {
  // TypeScript ensures 'email' exists and is a string
  console.log(`Sending email to ${user.email.toLowerCase()}`);
}
```

# type vs interface in TypeScript

Both `type` aliases and `interface` declarations can describe the shape of an object, but they have distinct capabilities.

---

## Comparison Table

| Feature | `interface` | `type` |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Declaration Merging** | Yes (merges duplicate interfaces) | No (duplicate names throw error) |
| **Primitives / Unions / Tuples** | No | Yes (`type ID = string \| number`) |
| **Extends Syntax** | `interface B extends A {}` | Intersection: `type B = A & {}` |
| **Performance** | Slightly faster compiler caching | Slightly higher resolution overhead |
| **Object Orientation** | Preferred for OOP & public library APIs | Preferred for unions, utilities, and complex transformations |

```typescript
// 1. Interface Declaration Merging (Extensible)
interface Box {
  height: number;
}
interface Box {
  width: number; // Merged into { height: number; width: number }
}

// 2. Type Aliases for Unions & Tuples
type Status = "pending" | "fulfilled" | "rejected";
type Point = [x: number, y: number];
```

# Extending Interfaces in TypeScript

Yes! Interfaces can extend other interfaces, type aliases (if they define an object shape), or even multiple interfaces simultaneously using the `extends` keyword.

```typescript
interface BaseEntity {
  id: string;
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
}

interface User extends BaseEntity {
  name: string;
  email: string;
  role: "admin" | "member";
}

// Multiple inheritance:
interface Printable {
  print(): void;
}

interface Serializable {
  serialize(): string;
}

interface DocumentModel extends Printable, Serializable {
  title: string;
}
```

# Union Types in TypeScript

A **Union Type** (`A | B`) models a value that can be **one of several types**.

```typescript
type ID = string | number;

function formatId(id: ID): string {
  // TypeScript requires narrowing before calling type-specific methods
  if (typeof id === "string") {
    return id.toUpperCase();
  }
  return id.toFixed(0);
}

type NetworkState = 
  | { state: "loading" }
  | { state: "success"; response: string }
  | { state: "error"; reason: string };
```

# Intersection Types in TypeScript

An **Intersection Type** (`A & B`) combines multiple types into one, requiring the resulting value to satisfy **all** constituent types simultaneously.

```typescript
interface Timestamped {
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
}

interface Identifiable {
  id: string;
}

type DatabaseRecord<T> = T & Identifiable & Timestamped;

interface Product {
  name: string;
  price: number;
}

const item: DatabaseRecord<Product> = {
  id: "prod_101",
  name: "Mechanical Keyboard",
  price: 150,
  createdAt: new Date(),
  updatedAt: new Date(),
};
```

# Type Guards in TypeScript

A **Type Guard** is an expression that performs a runtime check confirming the type of a variable in a specific scope.

---

## User-Defined Type Guards (`is`)

```typescript
interface Admin {
  id: string;
  role: "admin";
  permissions: string[];
}

interface Member {
  id: string;
  role: "member";
}

type Account = Admin | Member;

// Custom Type Guard predicate: 'user is Admin'
function isAdmin(user: Account): user is Admin {
  return (user as Admin).permissions !== undefined;
}

function processAccount(user: Account) {
  if (isAdmin(user)) {
    console.log(user.permissions.length); // Safely accessible!
  } else {
    console.log(user.role); // Inferred as Member
  }
}
```

# Discriminated Unions in TypeScript

A **Discriminated Union** (also called a Tagged Union or Algebraic Data Type) is a pattern where every type in a union shares a single common **discriminant literal property** (like `type` or `status`).

---

## Exhaustive Pattern Matching

```typescript
interface Circle {
  kind: "circle"; // Discriminant
  radius: number;
}

interface Square {
  kind: "square"; // Discriminant
  side: number;
}

interface Rectangle {
  kind: "rectangle"; // Discriminant
  width: number;
  height: number;
}

type Shape = Circle | Square | Rectangle;

function getArea(shape: Shape): number {
  switch (shape.kind) {
    case "circle":
      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;
    case "square":
      return shape.side ** 2;
    case "rectangle":
      return shape.width * shape.height;
    default:
      // Exhaustiveness check: compile error if a new shape is added and unhandled
      const _exhaustive: never = shape;
      throw new Error(`Unhandled shape: ${_exhaustive}`);
  }
}
```

# The void Type in TypeScript

`void` represents the **absence of a return value** from a function.

```typescript
function logMessage(msg: string): void {
  console.log(msg);
  // Returns undefined implicitly
}
```

> **Note on Callback Signatures:** When a callback is typed with return type `void` (e.g. `() => void`), it means "the caller will ignore any returned value", not that the callback is forbidden from returning a value.

# Structural Typing (Duck Typing) in TypeScript

TypeScript uses a **Structural Type System** rather than a Nominal Type System (like Java or C#).

Two types are considered compatible if they have the **same structure/shape**, regardless of their declaration names.

```typescript
interface Point2D {
  x: number;
  y: number;
}

class Vector2D {
  constructor(public x: number, public y: number) {}
}

function renderPoint(p: Point2D) {
  console.log(p.x, p.y);
}

const v = new Vector2D(10, 20);
renderPoint(v); // Perfectly valid! Vector2D matches the structure of Point2D.
```

# Literal Types in TypeScript

A **Literal Type** restricts a variable to an exact, specific primitive value rather than any general string, number, or boolean.

```typescript
type Direction = "north" | "south" | "east" | "west";
type HttpStatusCode = 200 | 201 | 400 | 401 | 404 | 500;
type Toggle = true | false;

let dir: Direction = "north";
// dir = "up"; // Error: Type '"up"' is not assignable to type 'Direction'.
```

# Enums and Their Modern Alternatives

TypeScript provides `enum` syntax, but modern TypeScript best practices frequently favor **const object maps with union types** instead.

---

## 1. Traditional Enum Issues
- Generates runtime JavaScript boilerplate objects.
- Numeric enums are not type-safe in older TS versions.

## 2. The Modern Alternative: `as const` Objects

```typescript
// Modern Preferred Pattern:
export const Role = {
  ADMIN: "admin",
  USER: "user",
  MODERATOR: "moderator",
} as const;

export type Role = (typeof Role)[keyof typeof Role]; // "admin" | "user" | "moderator"

function assignRole(role: Role) {
  console.log("Assigned:", role);
}

assignRole(Role.ADMIN); // "admin"
assignRole("user");     // Valid without enum imports!
```

# Tuples in TypeScript

A **Tuple** is an array of fixed length where each positional index has a known, specific type.

```typescript
// [latitude, longitude, locationName]
type GeoLocation = [latitude: number, longitude: number, name?: string];

const loc: GeoLocation = [37.7749, -122.4194, "San Francisco"];

// React useState returns a tuple:
type StateHook<T> = [state: T, setState: (val: T) => void];
```

# Optional Properties (`?`) in TypeScript

Appending `?` to a property name marks it as optional, allowing it to be omitted or set to `undefined`.

```typescript
interface UserProfile {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  avatarUrl?: string; // string | undefined
}

const u1: UserProfile = { id: "1", name: "Alice" }; // Valid
const u2: UserProfile = { id: "2", name: "Bob", avatarUrl: "https://..." }; // Valid
```

# readonly Properties in TypeScript

The `readonly` modifier prevents property re-assignment after object initialization.

```typescript
interface Config {
  readonly apiUrl: string;
  readonly port: number;
}

const config: Config = {
  apiUrl: "https://api.remit.dev",
  port: 8080,
};

// config.port = 3000; // Error: Cannot assign to 'port' because it is a read-only property.

// For deep immutability:
const deepConfig = {
  env: "production",
  db: { host: "localhost" }
} as const;
```

# Writing a Generic Function

```typescript
function getFirstElement<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined {
  return arr.length > 0 ? arr[0] : undefined;
}

const numArr = [10, 20, 30];
const firstNum = getFirstElement(numArr); // Inferred as: number | undefined

const strArr = ["apple", "banana"];
const firstStr = getFirstElement(strArr); // Inferred as: string | undefined
```

# Constraining Generics (`extends`)

Generic constraints restrict type parameters to types possessing specific properties using the `extends` keyword.

```typescript
interface HasLength {
  length: number;
}

function logWithLength<T extends HasLength>(item: T): T {
  console.log(`Length is: ${item.length}`);
  return item;
}

logWithLength("Hello");       // Valid (strings have .length)
logWithLength([1, 2, 3]);     // Valid (arrays have .length)
logWithLength({ length: 10 });// Valid
// logWithLength(123);        // Error: Argument of type 'number' is not assignable to 'HasLength'.
```

# The keyof Operator in TypeScript

The `keyof` operator extracts the **union of all public property names/keys** of an object type.

```typescript
interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  age: number;
}

type UserKeys = keyof User; // "id" | "name" | "age"

function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}

const user: User = { id: "u1", name: "Alice", age: 28 };
const nameVal = getProperty(user, "name"); // Inferred as: string
// getProperty(user, "invalidKey"); // Compile Error!
```

# Indexed Access Types (`T[K]`)

Indexed access types let you look up the specific type of a property on another type.

```typescript
interface AppConfig {
  database: {
    host: string;
    port: number;
    ssl: boolean;
  };
  auth: {
    secret: string;
    expiresIn: number;
  };
}

type DatabaseConfig = AppConfig["database"]; // { host: string; port: number; ssl: boolean; }
type PortType = AppConfig["database"]["port"]; // number
```

# The typeof Operator in TypeScript

In TypeScript, `typeof` can be used in **type space** to query and capture the static type of a runtime variable or object.

```typescript
const defaultSettings = {
  theme: "dark",
  fontSize: 14,
  showSidebar: true,
};

// Capture the type without manually authoring an interface
type Settings = typeof defaultSettings;
// Equivalent to: { theme: string; fontSize: number; showSidebar: boolean; }
```

# Generic Interfaces in TypeScript

```typescript
interface ApiResponse<TData, TMeta = Record<string, unknown>> {
  status: "success" | "error";
  statusCode: number;
  data: TData;
  meta?: TMeta;
}

interface UserData {
  id: string;
  email: string;
}

type UserResponse = ApiResponse<UserData>;
```

# Generic Classes in TypeScript

```typescript
class Queue<T> {
  private items: T[] = [];

  enqueue(item: T): void {
    this.items.push(item);
  }

  dequeue(): T | undefined {
    return this.items.shift();
  }

  size(): number {
    return this.items.length;
  }
}

const stringQueue = new Queue<string>();
stringQueue.enqueue("first");
```

# Generic Type Inference

When invoking a generic function, TypeScript automatically analyzes the arguments passed and **infers the type parameters** without requiring explicit `<T>` annotations.

```typescript
function pair<A, B>(first: A, second: B): [A, B] {
  return [first, second];
}

// Inferred automatically as: [string, number]
const result = pair("score", 100); 
```

# Designing a Generic API Response Type

```typescript
export type ApiResponse<T> =
  | {
      success: true;
      data: T;
      timestamp: string;
    }
  | {
      success: false;
      error: {
        code: string;
        message: string;
        details?: unknown;
      };
      timestamp: string;
    };
```

# Designing a Generic Repository Interface

```typescript
export interface IRepository<T, ID = string> {
  findById(id: ID): Promise<T | null>;
  findAll(filter?: Partial<T>): Promise<T[]>;
  create(item: Omit<T, "id" | "createdAt" | "updatedAt">): Promise<T>;
  update(id: ID, item: Partial<T>): Promise<T>;
  delete(id: ID): Promise<boolean>;
}
```

# Mapped Types in TypeScript

**Mapped Types** allow creating new types based on old ones by transforming each property in an existing type.

```typescript
type ReadonlyObject<T> = {
  readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K];
};

type OptionalObject<T> = {
  [K in keyof T]?: T[K];
};

type NullableObject<T> = {
  [K in keyof T]: T[K] | null;
};
```

# Conditional Types in TypeScript

Conditional types choose one of two possible types based on a condition expressed with the ternary `extends ? :` syntax.

```typescript
// T extends U ? TrueType : FalseType
type IsString<T> = T extends string ? "Yes" : "No";

type A = IsString<string>; // "Yes"
type B = IsString<number>; // "No"

// Unwrapping Promises with 'infer':
type AwaitedType<T> = T extends Promise<infer R> ? R : T;
```

# Template Literal Types in TypeScript

Template literal types build types by interpolating unions into string templates.

```typescript
type Event = "click" | "hover" | "focus";
type HandlerName = `on${Capitalize<Event>}`;
// Result: "onClick" | "onHover" | "onFocus"

type HttpMethod = "GET" | "POST" | "PUT" | "DELETE";
type Endpoint = `/api/${string}`;
type ApiRoute = `${HttpMethod} ${Endpoint}`;
// E.g.: "GET /api/users" | "POST /api/auth/login"
```

# Partial<T> in TypeScript

`Partial<T>` returns a type where **all properties of `T` are made optional**.

```typescript
// Implementation under the hood:
type CustomPartial<T> = {
  [P in keyof T]?: T[P];
};

interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

function updateUser(id: string, fields: Partial<User>) {
  // Can pass any subset of User properties!
}
```

# Required<T> in TypeScript

`Required<T>` makes **all optional properties in `T` required** by stripping the `?` modifier using `-?`.

```typescript
// Implementation:
type CustomRequired<T> = {
  [P in keyof T]-?: T[P];
};

interface Config {
  host: string;
  port?: number;
  timeout?: number;
}

const fullConfig: Required<Config> = {
  host: "localhost",
  port: 8080,
  timeout: 5000, // Error if omitted!
};
```

# Pick<T, K> in TypeScript

`Pick<T, K>` constructs a type by choosing a specific subset of keys `K` from type `T`.

```typescript
// Implementation:
type CustomPick<T, K extends keyof T> = {
  [P in K]: T[P];
};

interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  passwordHash: string;
  role: string;
}

// Client-safe user type:
type UserPreview = Pick<User, "id" | "name" | "email">;
```

# Omit<T, K> in TypeScript

`Omit<T, K>` constructs a type by removing a specific set of keys `K` from `T`.

```typescript
// Implementation:
type CustomOmit<T, K extends keyof any> = Pick<T, Exclude<keyof T, K>>;

interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  passwordHash: string;
}

type UserWithoutPassword = Omit<User, "passwordHash">;
```

# Record<K, T> in TypeScript

`Record<K, T>` constructs an object type whose property keys are `K` and whose property values are `T`.

```typescript
// Implementation:
type CustomRecord<K extends keyof any, T> = {
  [P in K]: T;
};

type Role = "admin" | "editor" | "viewer";

interface RolePermissions {
  canRead: boolean;
  canWrite: boolean;
  canDelete: boolean;
}

const permissionsMap: Record<Role, RolePermissions> = {
  admin: { canRead: true, canWrite: true, canDelete: true },
  editor: { canRead: true, canWrite: true, canDelete: false },
  viewer: { canRead: true, canWrite: false, canDelete: false },
};
```

# Readonly<T> in TypeScript

`Readonly<T>` marks all properties of `T` as immutable.

```typescript
type CustomReadonly<T> = {
  readonly [P in keyof T]: T[P];
};
```

# Creating Custom Utility Types

```typescript
// Make only specific keys K optional in T:
type OptionalKeys<T, K extends keyof T> = Omit<T, K> & Partial<Pick<T, K>>;

interface Article {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  content: string;
  publishedAt: Date;
}

// 'publishedAt' becomes optional, others remain required:
type DraftArticle = OptionalKeys<Article, "publishedAt">;
```

# Modeling Success/Error Responses with Discriminated Unions

```typescript
type Result<T, E = Error> =
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; error: E };

async function fetchUser(id: string): Promise<Result<{ id: string; name: string }>> {
  try {
    const user = await db.findUser(id);
    return { success: true, data: user };
  } catch (err: any) {
    return { success: false, error: err };
  }
}
```

# Safely Typing External API Data

Never cast external JSON payloads directly using `as DataType`. Use runtime validation libraries like **Zod** to validate and infer static types simultaneously.

```typescript
import { z } from "zod";

const UserSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string().uuid(),
  email: z.string().email(),
  age: z.number().min(18).optional(),
});

// Infer static TypeScript type directly from runtime schema:
type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;

async function getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  const json = await res.json();
  return UserSchema.parse(json); // Throws if shape does not match!
}
```

# Typing Express Middleware in TypeScript

```typescript
import { Request, Response, NextFunction } from "express";

export interface AuthenticatedRequest extends Request {
  user?: {
    id: string;
    role: string;
  };
}

export function authMiddleware(
  req: AuthenticatedRequest,
  res: Response,
  next: NextFunction
) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization;
  if (!token) return res.status(401).json({ error: "Unauthorized" });
  req.user = { id: "u123", role: "admin" };
  next();
}
```

# Typing Express Request & Response Generics

Express `Request` accepts 5 generic parameters:
`Request<Params, ResBody, ReqBody, ReqQuery, Locals>`

```typescript
import { Request, Response } from "express";

interface UpdateUserParams {
  userId: string;
}

interface UpdateUserBody {
  name?: string;
  email?: string;
}

interface ApiResponse {
  success: boolean;
  message: string;
}

export async function updateUserHandler(
  req: Request<UpdateUserParams, ApiResponse, UpdateUserBody>,
  res: Response<ApiResponse>
) {
  const { userId } = req.params; // string
  const { name, email } = req.body; // string | undefined
  return res.json({ success: true, message: `Updated ${userId}` });
}
```

# Typing a Type-Safe Event Emitter

```typescript
type EventMap = {
  "user:login": { userId: string; timestamp: Date };
  "order:created": { orderId: string; amount: number };
  "disconnect": void;
};

class TypedEventEmitter {
  private listeners = new Map<string, Function[]>();

  on<K extends keyof EventMap>(event: K, listener: (payload: EventMap[K]) => void) {
    const list = this.listeners.get(event) || [];
    list.push(listener);
    this.listeners.set(event, list);
  }

  emit<K extends keyof EventMap>(event: K, payload: EventMap[K]) {
    const list = this.listeners.get(event) || [];
    list.forEach(fn => fn(payload));
  }
}
```

# Typing a Reusable Database Repository

```typescript
interface BaseEntity {
  id: string;
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
}

abstract class BaseRepository<T extends BaseEntity> {
  abstract findById(id: string): Promise<T | null>;
  abstract create(data: Omit<T, keyof BaseEntity>): Promise<T>;
}
```

# Practical: Generic getProperty() Function

```typescript
function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}

const user = { id: "101", username: "tony", active: true };
const u = getProperty(user, "username"); // string
```

# Practical: Make Selected Properties Optional

```typescript
type MakeOptional<T, K extends keyof T> = Omit<T, K> & Partial<Pick<T, K>>;

interface Product {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  price: number;
  description: string;
}

type CreateProductDTO = MakeOptional<Product, "id" | "description">;
```

# Practical: Extract Keys of a Particular Value Type

```typescript
type KeysMatching<T, V> = {
  [K in keyof T]: T[K] extends V ? K : never;
}[keyof T];

interface UserProfile {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  age: number;
  balance: number;
  isVerified: boolean;
}

type NumericKeys = KeysMatching<UserProfile, number>; // "age" | "balance"
```

# Practical: Type-Safe API Response Pattern

```typescript
type ApiResponse<T> =
  | { success: true; data: T; error?: never }
  | { success: false; data?: never; error: { code: string; message: string } };
```

# Practical: Type-Safe Event Emitter

```typescript
type EventCallback<T> = (data: T) => void;

class SafeEmitter<TEvents extends Record<string, any>> {
  private events: { [K in keyof TEvents]?: EventCallback<TEvents[K]>[] } = {};

  on<K extends keyof TEvents>(event: K, cb: EventCallback<TEvents[K]>) {
    this.events[event] = this.events[event] || [];
    this.events[event]!.push(cb);
  }

  emit<K extends keyof TEvents>(event: K, data: TEvents[K]) {
    this.events[event]?.forEach(cb => cb(data));
  }
}
```

# Practical: Generic Pagination Response

```typescript
interface PaginatedResponse<T> {
  items: T[];
  pagination: {
    totalItems: number;
    totalPages: number;
    currentPage: number;
    pageSize: number;
    hasNextPage: boolean;
    hasPrevPage: boolean;
  };
}
```

# Practical: Type-Safe Repository Interface

```typescript
export interface BaseEntity {
  id: string;
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
}

export interface IAsyncRepository<T extends BaseEntity> {
  findById(id: string): Promise<T | null>;
  findMany(filter?: Partial<T>): Promise<T[]>;
  create(entity: Omit<T, "id" | "createdAt" | "updatedAt">): Promise<T>;
  update(id: string, patch: Partial<Omit<T, "id" | "createdAt">>): Promise<T>;
  delete(id: string): Promise<boolean>;
}
```
